<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e>
      a       zoo:Oeuvre ;
      zoo:author "SEMONIDES AMORGINUS" ;
      zoo:date "date not found" ;
      zoo:editor "Unknown editor" ;
      zoo:file "./zoo/zoo48/1e.xml" ;
      zoo:identifier "elegy_and_iambus_semonides" ;
      zoo:title "Elegy and Iambus Semonides" .


<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/2>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 2 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Were he to die we should not take it to heart, if we were wise, for more than a single day.or now that he is dead we shall not ... if we are wise ... (cf. Theogn. passim )" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/28>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 28 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "The city of Oenopion is valued not only by her vines but by the works of the sons of Archermus." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/16>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 16 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "... There is also a representation of Ajax dragging Cassandra from the image of Athena, thus inscribed:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/15>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 15 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Hermes here showeth Hera and Athena and Aphrodite unto Alexander for to judge of their beauty." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/51>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 51 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "victuals" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/68>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 68 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "the sturdy grinding-wench busy at the mill" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/63>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 63 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "starving" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/71>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 71 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "After many pleasant games with them of like age, I that grew from earth have become earth again,cf. Theogn. 878 and my name is Aristocles of the Peiraeus, son of Menon." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/44>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 44 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "... which is why we say:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/56>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 56 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "'Tis wrong that dear comrades should refrain long from the revel; for of such is the recollection most delightful." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/20>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 20 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "'Tis but of little learning;" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/32>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 32 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "He would not have drunk the hellebore had he been in love.the translation merely gives the English of the Greek" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Marpessa the fair-ankled whom Apollo had taken from him is brought back again by Idas from the temple a willing follower." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/3>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 3 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "There's much time for us to lie dead in, yet we live years few in number and live them ill." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/39>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 39 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "The statuea bronze statue of Heracles dedicated at Olympia by the Thasians bears the following couplet:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/27>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 27 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "They afterwards made many statues in the islands adjoining Chios, for instance in Delos, beneath which they inscribed lines saying:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/26>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 26 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Man who walkest the way thinking of other things, stand and pity when thou seest the tomb of Thrason." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/14>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 14 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "And there is Hermes, too, bringing the Goddesses to be judged for their beauty by Alexanderi.e. Paris son of Priam; the inscription runs thus:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/79>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 79 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "There were two very famous sculptors called Damophilus and Gorgasus, who were also painters, and who adorned the temple of Ceresdedicated 493 B.C. near the Circus Maximum at Rome with both kinds of their art, inscribing their works with Greek verses to signify that on the right they were made by Damophilus, on the left by Gorgasus.there are perh. more fragments of the Iambic Poets among the Tragic Fragments collected by Nauck, but it is impossible to distinguish them" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/40>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 40 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "But there (stood?) a wondrous Tromilean cheese from Achaia which they had brought down." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/52>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 52 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "not what you eat at home but what you eat on a journey, compare the Odyssey (2.410) and Semonides.citation lost, but we at least know that S. used the word" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/55>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 55 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "A snake and a lion in the hills a hunter may hear and see when he avoideth them, for their will and likewise their intent may be learnt; but a man hideth one thing in his heart and speaketh another; his words are smooth and gentle, but his works an enemy's." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/64>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 64 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "or fasting; so Semonides ... according to Herodian in his Homophones." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/67>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 67 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "I do not deem thee worthy of little, yet I have not much.i.e. to give thee" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/70>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 70 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Of all that 'tis right for happy mortals to enjoy I partook in life and share in death; when I died I left regret for my youth and memory of my virtue; and my name is Lonchis." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/31>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 31 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "and:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/43>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 43 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "If Southwind call up North, A storm will come forth.Plut. Prim. Frig. 11 gives the 2nd line as ‘Snow will soon come forth’" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/4>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 4 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "No man is altogether without blame nor without harm.meaning of a)kh/rios uncertain, but as a)/mwmos refers to what he does, it prob. refers to what happens to him; lit. unaffected by the *kh=res or Doom-Spirits (cf. i. 21)" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/59>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 59 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Begone, ye Spirits,the Gk. word generally means Doom-Spirits 'tis no more the Anthesteria;" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/38>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 38 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Phayllus leapt five-and-fifty feet and threw the quoit five-and-ninety.cf. Sch. Ar. Ach. 214, Sch. Plat. Cratyl. 413 a, Phot. Lex. u(pe/r ta/ e)skamme/na ; the Pythian victor who commanded the Crotoniates' single ship at Salamis, cf. Hdt. 8.47, Paus. 10.9.2" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/37>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 37 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Phayllus:—. . Five-event champion, upon whom was written the following inscription:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/25>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 25 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "I am the tomb of Myrrhina who died of the plague." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/13>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 13 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This is men's Fear and he that holdeth him Agamemnon." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/90>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 90 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "used of those who try to play the part of a deceiver." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/46>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 46 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "When South comes on Or when North's nigh done." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/41>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 41 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "And the pig, how I singed it and cut it up in ritual fashion; I'm no 'prentice." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/58>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 58 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "There's many a friendship lost for lack of speaking.cf. Apost. 14. 59 a" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/22>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 22 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "a cheating watcher of the fig-drying" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/53>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 53 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "bran" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2/text/10>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 10 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "in the Iambi." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/65>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 65 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "drying-crate" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/34>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 34 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Now clear means ‘Delos,’ which means ‘clear’ and is surrounded by the sea, and mother means ‘Leto,’ who was daughter of Coeus , which is the Macedonian word for ‘number.’cf. Eust. 1558.3" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/10>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 10 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Good though he be, he has met a better man." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/5>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 5 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "to run like a sucking foal beside his mother" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/49>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 49 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This city and people of Erechtheus mourn those who died, sons of the men of Athens, in the vanguard before Potidaea, exchanging their lives for prowess and giving fame unto the land of their birth.one of three inscriptions on those who died before Potidaea in 430 B.C." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/48>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 48 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Standing thus beside Alpheus Pelasgus a-boxing once showed with his hands the rules of Polydeuces when he was proclaimed victor; and now, O Father Zeus, hand Thou unto Arcady again a noble fame, and honour Philippus, who here did throw four boys of the Isles in straight fight.cf. Paus. 8. 1. 2 (‘Pelasgus first king of Arcadia’), 6. 8. 5 (‘Philippus the Azanian of Pellana won the boxing for boys .. the statue is by Myron’)" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/36>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 36 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "The Megarians set me up here in obedience to the Delphic Voice as a far-seen memorial to the shrewd Orsippus, who freed the furthest bounds of his country from foemen who ravaged it far and wide, and was the first Greek to be crowned at Olympia naked for victory in the race-course where before men had gone girded.he won in the 15th Olympiad = 720 B.C., but the orig. inscr. was prob. not so old" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/24>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 24 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "The tomb of Phrasicleia; I shall ever be called maiden, because the Gods ordained me that name instead of marriage." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/12>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 12 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "and that on the shield of Agamemnon:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/66>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 66 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "for figs.... also in the form tarsia/ (i.e. tarsih/ ], which occurs in Semonides." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/57>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 57 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Give ear to my prayer, great Nursing-Mother, and grant that this woman may reject the love and bed of the young; let her rather rejoice in old men of hoary temples, whose strength may be blunted but their heart desireth.cf. Vit. Hom. p. 15, Eust. 1968. 41" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/30>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 30 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "and I anointed myself with unguents and spices and baccaris; for a merchant was there." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/69>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 69 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "and house-slavesi.e. slaves born in the house and serfs, are transported with joy and gladness." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/33>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 33 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "In clear was I born, my birthplace is in the midst of the brine, and my mother is the daughter of number." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/42>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 42 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "No man gave (me) so much as a cup of wine-lees." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/54>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 54 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "in Semonides." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/45>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 45 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "If Northwind find mire, A storm will be nigher.cf. Theophr. Vent. 46" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/21>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 21 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "that is, easy to learn.the descriptive genitive belies an early date" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/3/text/2>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 2 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/3> ;
      zoo:text "Time's first and last of all things unto men, Hath all things in 't, is one and not-one ever, Is here when past 'tis, past when it is here, Inside itself it is and thence to itself Runs counter,lit. out of itself being inside itself it runs an opposite way to itself [ au)tw=| = au(tw=| and does double duty) and what seems to-day, in sooth Is yesterday, and yesterday to-day.the Epigrams ascribed to S. in the Anthology (12.22 and 232) are certainly by a later handFor the Iambic fragments of Chares, who, as possibly pre-Alexandrian, might be included in this book, see Powell Collectanea Alexandrina , p. 223." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/6>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 6 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "craving almost to be one with his good friend." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/37>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 37 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Died of earth of belt when he lost pots;" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/49>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 49 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Eat mint nor sow't When war's afoot." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/59>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 59 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "If there were a city made of such men as these, her enemies could never dedicate a trophy unto Zeus. Swift Ares slew Aristocritus as he won fame with spear and arm from an enemy.cf. Aesch. Ag. 110" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/47>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 47 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Tell, hand-wrought voice of stone, who set this offering up to grace Apollo's altar. —If thou wouldst have me say, 'twas Panamyes son of Casbollis who dedicated this, for a tithe unto the God." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/80>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 80 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Sow wheat in mire, 'Twill grow the higher; Sow barley in dust, 'Twill never know rustthe ref. is not to heavy and light soil, but to wet and dry; cf. the previous chapter of Plut." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/35>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 35 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This memorial of his rule was set up by Peisistratus son of Hippias in the close of Pythian Apollo.the stone was found in 1877; this grandson of the great Peisistratus was archon some time between 523 and 510 B.C." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/23>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 23 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "When you look on the monument of Cleoetas son of Menesaechmus who died a boy, pity him for dying so beautiful." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/92>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 92 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "and:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/11>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 11 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This is Iphidamas; Coon fighteth for him;" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/55>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 55 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "deception" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/67>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 67 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "baldhead" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/24>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 24 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Here I leave my sins behind, Here the better way I find;" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/36>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 36 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "For less is ‘worse’ and worse is ‘cheiron,’ and suffered is ‘made,’ and it means that Cheiron the centaur brought up Achilles. And again:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/31>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 31 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "and I let myself out bylit. shut myself from the back staircase-door." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/12>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 12 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "There's God after all, or someone stronger; we shall succeed." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/43>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 43 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "He took away the table whither he had taken the cups.lit. by the path by which, etc.; or to which the cups ..; the tops of Gk. tables were removable" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/7>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 7 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "A man wins himself nothing whatsoever that is better than a good wife nor worse than a bad." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/48>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 48 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "When figs are begun Or pumpkins nigh done." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "but the dried figs of the white-fig tree apart" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/58>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 58 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "No mortal man hath ever found a better art for the training of the soul for the contests of virtue than Gorgias, whose image is dedicated in the vale of Apollo to prove not his wealth but his piety.cf. Paus. 6. 17. 7, Plat. Gorg. 58, 488; Plin. N.H. 34. 4 says that G. was the first to dedicate a statue of himself in solid gold at Delphi; this is the 2nd of two inscriptions under the above title; the first implies that they were both inscribed by G.'s grandson Eumolpus" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/91>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 91 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "What is it that we all teach without knowing how to do it?" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/46>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 46 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "The temple hath a golden eweri.e. the shield which the Spartans and their allies dedicated as a gift from Tanagra, being the tithe of their spoils of the Argives, Athenians, and Ionians, because of their victory in the war.cf. Simon. 135. L.G. ; the Spartans and Boeotians defeated Athens and her allies at Tanagra in Boeotia in 457 B.C." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/34>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 34 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "These works were made by Eutelidas and Chrysothemis of Argos, who had learnt their art from them that went before. or, emending the Gk. , their fathers" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/44>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 44 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "But this is a taper-footed Argive cup;" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/22>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 22 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "I am a post upon the tomb of Xenares son of Meixis." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/10>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 10 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "So runs the verse; and there is Coon fighting with Agamemnon for the corpse of Iphidamas son of Antenor; and Agamemnon's shield is blazoned with Fear with a lion's head. The inscription on Iphidamas is this:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/56>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 56 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "in Semonides." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/68>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 68 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "in Semonides." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/35>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 35 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Less having suffered the son of Thetis;" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/47>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 47 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "'Ware clouds from the sea In winter wild, 'Ware clouds from the shore In summer mild.cf. Ibid. 25. 7" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/20>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 20 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "like an eel in the sedimentthe oily sediment of a public bath" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/11>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 11 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "the mss add ‘from Euripides' Telephus ,’ which can hardly be correct Be patient, albeit the Gods give thee a hard lot." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/23>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 23 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Meet in little and meet in great." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/32>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 32 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "and with a swaggering gait like a horse with arching neck." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/8>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 8 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "In the beginning God made woman's mind apart from man's.or made mind apart from woman" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/39>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 39 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Northwind o' nights Ne'er sees three lights.cf. Arist. Prob. 26. 9, 14" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/15>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 15 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Thus doing one most righteous deed among his unrighteous.cf. Polyb. 4, 18, 15, 25 (which prove this the right translation)" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/27>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 27 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "But a good man is sometimes bad and sometimes good.cf. Xen. Mem. i. 2. 20" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/82>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 82 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Gross bellies do not make fine wits. Old fools do not make young wiseacres." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/94>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 94 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "... the answer to the latter is ‘Time,’ which is the same for all and is nowhere, because it has not its nature (or existence) in one place; and to the former ‘preservation of life,’ because everyone teaches it to his neighbour without knowing how to do it in his own case." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/69>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 69 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "in archaistic lettersHere lies Polyxene, who hath left sorrow with her wedded husband and her mother and the father that begat her." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/57>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 57 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Resolved by the tribe Cecropis in the archonship of Charisander375 B.C. by secret vote of the tribesmen in a general meeting in the Acropolis. Onetor son of Cephisodorus of Melite said: ‘Whereas Pyrrhas..hath distinguished himself on behalf of the tribe and the city, it is resolved to thank him for his goodness and to crown him with a gold crown worth 500 drachmas, etc.’" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/70>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 70 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Put your foot upon his neck, bring him to the ground;" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/45>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 45 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This wayside tomb shall be called the tomb of Procleidas, who died fighting for his country." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/33>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 33 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "either blind or blear-eyed or leering.last word doubtful: if rightly emended it means ‘with lewd looks’" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/33>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 33 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Damaretus of Heraea and his son and grandson each won two victories at Olympia, Damaretus [in the race in armour] in the 65th Olympiad (519 B.C.), when that race was first introduced, and likewise in the 66th —the statue holds a shield, as in our own day, and also wears a helmet and greaves, which later were disused in the race both by the Eleans and the other Greeks —and Theopompus son of Damaretus and his son of like name, the former in the five-events and the latter in wrestling. Who made the statue of Theopompus wrestler we do not know; but those of his father and grandfather, according to the inscription, are the work of the Argives Eutelidas and Chryso themis. The trainers are not mentioned. The inscription runs thus:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/21>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 21 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This is the monument of Tlasias son of Menecrates of Oeanthe, made for him by the people because he was a proxenus dear to them but perished at sea, and this city is sore with a public sorrow. And it was his brother Praximenes who came from his native land and fashioned this monument together with the people." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/45>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 45 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "but some writers explain it as one that has his head tapering [ o)cu/s ]towards his eyes [ fa/h ]. (b) foco/s is properly applied to fire-cracked pottery, being as it were floco/s or flamy. a variant is faoco/s , which apparently comes from the first etymology" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/57>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 57 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "tumour" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/69>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 69 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "... ye sleep in ... like Lesbian Pryliscf. Sch. Lycophr. 219 and Milne Cat. Lit. Pap. Brit. Mus. 53 p. 40, a frag. too mutilated to be included here ..." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/14>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 14 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "For the good are good in one way, and the bad bad in many." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/21>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 21 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "and the accusative:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/9>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 9 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "One made He of a bristly Sow; all that is in her house lies disorderly, defiled with dirt, and rolling upon the floor, and she groweth fat a-sitting among the middens in garments as unwashed as herself." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/26>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 26 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "This saying is prescribed in the case of those who divine in themselves a change for the better. It seems that it was the custom at Athenian weddings for a child, whose parents were both living, to be crowned with thistles and acorns and to carry round (the bridal pair) a winnowing-fan full of loaves, saying, ‘Here I leave,’ etc.; signifying that they rejected the old wild way of life and had found that of civilisation.or civilised fare; cf. Hesych. e)/qugon, ktl ." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/38>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 38 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "meaning Aias (also = of earth ) son of Telamon (also = of belt ) died when he lost his arms (also = pots )." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/93>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 93 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "What is the same nowhere and everywhere?" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/68>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 68 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "The man who lies here bears the name of a ram, but he had the soul of a very righteous man.cf. Sosith. 2 Nauck" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/81>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 81 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "... who once did a thousand at bibasis , the most ever done." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/56>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 56 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Pure should he be that entereth the fragrant temple, and purity is to have righteous thoughts.cf. Porphyr. Abst. 3; the date of the temple is c. 380 B.C." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/44>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 44 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Hiero son of Deinomenes and the Syracusans (dedicated) unto Zeus (these) Etruscan spoils from Cumae.perh. the originally suggested form of the inser. was a simple hexameter (omitting o( ] and ‘and the Syracusans,’ an insertion which spoilt the metre: cf. the Spartans and Pausanias' inser. on the Plataean offering, Thuc. i. 132 (see L.G. ii. p. 352); Hiero defeated the Etruscans off Cumae in 474 B.C." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/22>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 22 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "For a heron that hath found a hawk eating a Maeandrian eel hath taken it from him.prob. metaphorical, but it might be the beginning of a Fable" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/32>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 32 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "To thee, O Love, thou maker of divers devices, did Charmus first set up this altar upon the shady bounds of the wrestling-school.cf. Paus. i. 30. 1 [ bwmo/s *e)/rwtos ]. Plut. Sol. i. [ a)/galma tou= *e)/rwtos ]" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/34>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 34 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "They sacrifice to the Nymphs and to the offspring of Maia;Hermes for these have honouremendation doubtful of shepherd folk." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/46>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 46 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "waggled the hoofs of his hind-legsfrom a Fable?" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/20>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 20 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "The life of this Byzesthere is some mistake either in transmission or on the part of P.'s informant ( sic ) lies in the reigns of Alyattes of Lydia and Astyages son of Cyaxares of Media.c. 570 B.C." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/58>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 58 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "in Semonides' 2nd Book." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/13>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 13 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "The Gods have not granted to all men to possess all things." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/25>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 25 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "and it was your pride that no one ever emitted that holy ululation so powerfully as yourself.translated by the brothers Vince, Loeb Library" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/10>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 10 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Another did God make of a knavish Vixen, a woman knowing in all things, who taketh note of all, be it bad or good; for the bad often calleth she good and the good bad; and she hath now this mood and now that." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Even as amid urchin-foot and spiny rest-harrowprickly plants spring the blooms of the soft white gillyflower.cf. Plut. Aud. Poet. 3, Frat. Am. 13, Ath. 3. 97 d" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2/text/4>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 4 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "These, according to Nicander of Thyateira, are the Heracleans who live at the foot of Mt. Oeta, being so named from a certain Lydian called Cylix (cup), who was one of the comrades who fought with Heracles." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/17>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 17 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "and we to the Sea of Helle sailed away.Vict. supposses Helles-pontus to be separated by the pause, but this is not really necessary" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/29>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 29 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "As wolves a lamb so lovers love the fair.cf. Hermog. Rh. Gr. 3. 321 W. Sch. Hermog. 5. 487" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/8>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 8 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "... The Chest also depicts the Dioscuri, one of them still beardless, and between them Helen, at whose feet lies Aethra daughter of Pittheus, in black garments. The inscription is an hexameter line with one word over:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/59>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 59 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "begging priest" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/60>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 60 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "referring to the souls (of the departed) who haunt the city at that festival. The proverb is used of those who expect to get the same things always.this sentence only in Zen." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/72>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 72 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Bow-wow you say, like a dog.perh. choliambic" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/67>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 67 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "said to be, or to have been, in the wall of the Propylaea, but doubtless originally at the Altar of the Twelve Gods in the agora; not in the new Corpus How far it is you shall know, since you have a true record which shows the length of your journey by letters; from the harbour to the Altar of the Twelve Gods is five-and-fifty furlongs.rather over 6 miles" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/55>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 55 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "By reason of their prowess the people of Athens' ancient land hath honoured with crowns the men who at peril of their lives first set about to overthrow them that had ruled the city with unjust ordinances." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/11>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 11 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Another of a Bitch, a busybodythe mss have litorgo/n , a form which occurs nowhere else and is prob. corrupt.; two ancient glosses prob. belong here; one, li/targon , explained by ‘running fast,’ seems etymologically unsound, the other, litourgo/n [ leitourgo/n ?], explained by ‘scoundrelly,’ can hardly have that meaning; but the latter, besides its official use as ‘doer of public works,’ may have had the meaning given above like her mother, one that would fain hear all, know all, and peering and prying everywhere barketh e'en though she see nothing; a man cannot check her with threats, no, not if in anger he dash her teeth out with a stone, nor yet though he speak gently with her, even though she be sitting among strangers —she must needs keep up her idle baying." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/23>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 23 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Why have I let my story run to a long tale?" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/43>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 43 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This memorial of his prowess was set up unto Lord Poseidon by Pausanias the ruler of spacious Greece." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/31>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 31 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Now Charmus (the polemarch) is said to have been the lover of Hippias, and the original builder of the Altar of Love next the Academy, which bears the inscription:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/35>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 35 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "cast forth with sodden clothes" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/47>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 47 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "looking for an octopus" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/2>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 2 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Youth is ever headstrong, but if it harm the Right, then is it a thing far worse." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/84>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 84 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "seems partly false and partly true; for no one is happy against his will, whereas misery is voluntary." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/96>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 96 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "A lisping lecher, short and bald and fat, Whose whoring's cheap, the Stagirite is that." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2/text/5>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 5 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "Quick, get the shoes on, and then step out opulently like this, with a lordly and delicate strut:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/28>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 28 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "We have both come to our senses at long last." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/16>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 16 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "The watchman should watch and the lover love.i.e. you can't do two things at once" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/9>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 9 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "The sons of Tyndareus bring Helen, and carry off Aethra from Aphidna." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/71>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 71 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "and other such provocative speeches, whereby some transplant so unhappily the spirit of the women's quarters to the dining-hall." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/48>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 48 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "burnt thigh-bonesthese, wrapped in some of the fat, were the parts of the victim which the Greeks burnt at a sacrifice, the rest being eaten by the company" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/83>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 83 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "No man fares ill for choice, nor well perforce," .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/66>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 66 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Aristotle son of Nicomachus, the expert in all knowledge, was here set up by the divine Alexander.see n. 4; or, keeping the text , The divine Aristotle, etc. was set up by Alexander (i.e. the infant son of Al. the Great, the real dedicator being Antipater)" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/12>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 12 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Another the Olympians fashioned of Earth, and gave to her husband all wanting in wits; such a woman knoweth neither evil nor good; her only art is to eat; and never though God give a bad winter draweth she her stool nigher the fire for the cold." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/54>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 54 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This tripod was dedicated for a tithe, as he had vowed, by Polymnestus' dear son Pyres unto Tritoborn Pallas. Made by Cresilas of Cydonia." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/42>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 42 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "... set up from the Medes as an offering to the God Apollo.this line was substituted by the Spartans for the eulogy of Pausanias (Simon. 16, 7 L.G. ); the grammatical subject is the list of cities on the ‘Serpent Column’ now at Constantinople" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/24>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 24 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "where a)ne/dramon stands for a)nadramei=n me/llw." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/36>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 36 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "that is, wet through." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/30>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 30 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This is the tomb of Deinias slain by the ruthless sea." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/3>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 3 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Such are the ills which fall to the lot of man;reading doubtful thick is he swathed about with dooms, and vain it is even for a barley-awn to try to enter.the metaphor seems to be taken from an infant so tightly swathed that not even the beard of a barley-ear (such as all of us who are country-bred have sometimes put up another's sleeve, to climb to his discomfort) can get between the folds" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/95>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 95 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Knead the peeled barley-corn and drink its juice." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/19>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 19 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "maidens stepping light and high.in the dance" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/6>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 6 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Moreover there is Atlas holding up Heaven and Earth upon his shoulders as in the story, and bringing the apples of the Hesperides. And who the man is who is coming towards him with the sword is not explained by any particular inscription, but he is clearly Heracles. The following is the inscription on the whole scene:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2/text/2>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 2 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "He's built without clamps, only with the u(pagwgei=s of his own habits.cf. Hesych. u(pagwgeu/s ; but the ancient explanation is doubtful; u(pag. is more likely to mean the tool with which the stones were brought flush with one another, i.e. he is as it were building with stones laid ‘dry’ and without clamps" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/37>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 37 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "laden with sodden clothes" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/49>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 49 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "the other benches are fullthe etymologist appears to have taken this to mean ‘the other timbers have drawn nigh,’ but there must be some mistake" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/50>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 50 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Toss pot on high But let love go by." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/65>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 65 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "..nor did the sinking scales of Fortune overwhelm thee shunning the baleful outrage of a foe; nay, self-willed came Death's end upon thee, as upon few of the heroes before thee. Wherefore to honour theethe Gk. suggests ‘as a son honours his father’ the world-renowned God Alexander hath set up here the guide of his wisdom.prob. put up to Aristotle by a Macedonian (hardly Antipater, who disapproved of the deification of Alexander) or on the proposal of a pro-Macedonian Athenian about 320; Alexander, who died before Aristotle, being a God is immortal; not in the new Corpus ; stone now lost" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/53>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 53 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Made by Euphron a Parian of no mean skill." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/13>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 13 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Another of the Sea, whose thoughts are in two minds; one day she laughs and is gay —a stranger seeing her within will praise her, saying ‘There's no better wife in all the world, nay, nor comelier’; the next she is intolerable to behold or draw nigh to, for then she rageth unapproachably, like a bitch with young; implacable and nasty is she to all, alike foe and friend. Even as the sea in summertime often will stand calm and harmless, to the great joy of the mariners, yet often will rage and toss with roaring waves, most like unto it is such a woman in disposition, nor hath the ocean a nature of other sort than hers. or keeping the Greek but the ocean hat a different nature" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/25>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 25 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "like to the egg of a Maeandrian goose" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/41>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 41 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Euchidas to Pytho ran and back the selfsame day." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/4>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 4 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Forms such as nhu+/s and grhu+/s with diaeresis are not declined in Ionic save that they have an accusative and a vocative, as in" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/86>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 86 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "When wheels turn, one half's up and t' other 's down." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/62>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 62 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "(his) seat is a chair, a well-matched possession. or, without emendation , marvel; ref. to the two parts of the chair, which was like a camp-stool: cf. Hesych. blw=sis" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/74>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 74 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "growing old within the house" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2/text/3>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 3 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "I have come afoot into the spleen-yardsprob. a play on oi)no/peda ‘vineyards,’ Cylicranes being taken to mean kulikokranei=s ‘cup-mixers,’ and the whole thing means ‘I am suffering from a debauch’ cf. Hesych. *kulikra/nwn and Scyth. p. 244 of the Cylicranes, and so beheld the beautiful city of Heraclea." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/3/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/3> ;
      zoo:text "which the beauteous Apollo attuneth unto Zeus comprising the beginning and end of every thing and hath for quill the bright light of the Sun." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/18>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 18 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "For if we pronounce Hellespontus as one word, it escapes the ear, so that it is not felt as a verse at all." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/7>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 7 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This is Atlas upholding the Heavens and about to give up the apples." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/26>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 26 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "forthwith plunging among lit. putting on like a garment, but the reading may be wrong the entrails like a kite" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/38>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 38 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Many the things thou dost finish, Telembrotus, ere ..." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/61>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 61 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "to Xanthe, that ancient crone so dear to many women" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/64>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 64 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This dust doth cover the body of Herodotus son of Lyxes, lord of the ancient Ionian lore, who sprang of a town of Dorians;Halicarnassus for to escape their monstrous blame he made Thurii his fatherland.cf. Sch. Tz. Chil. i. 19; not earlier than c. 330 B.C." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/52>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 52 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Execias both made and painted me." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/14>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 14 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Another's made of a stubbornreading doubtful and belaboured She-Ass; everything she doeth is hardly done, of necessity and after threats, and then 'tis left unfinished; meanwhile eateth she day in day out, in bower and in hall, and all men alike are welcome to her bed." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/40>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 40 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "I am the very work of Onatas son of Micon who had his dwelling in Aegina." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/5>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 5 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "The Thrian Nymphs who inspire the aged crow" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/97>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 97 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "there was a distinction between pallakh/ and e(tai/ra" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/73>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 73 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Hence the verb bau/zw ‘to bark.’" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/85>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 85 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Untimely love is all as good as hate." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/4>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 4 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "And the Muses are depicted singing and Apollo leading the song,i.e. dancing lyre in hand and the inscription is:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2/text/8>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 8 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "so that it would seem there is a mistake in one or other of these passages." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/15>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 15 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Another of a Cat, a woeful and miserable sort; for in her there's nought of fair or lovely or pleasant or desirable; she is woodmad for a love-mate, and yet when she hath him turneth his stomach; she doeth her neighbours much harm underhand, and often eateth up unaccepted offerings.cf. Ath. 5. 179 d [ p. th=s a)ko/smou gunako/s ]" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/27>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 27 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "‘What a creature is this that hath flown to us! it hath the worst life of all living things’.Fable of Zeus and the Crab? cf. Sch. Il. 18. 407 ‘said the Crab’" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/39>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 39 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "and he says of it:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/6>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 6 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "In time of discord even the villain receiveth honour.cf. Zenob. 3. 77, Plut. Vit. Nic. 11, Lyc. et Sull. 1, Alex. 53" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/63>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 63 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Far from their country these drew sword and put to rout the pride of the foe;these words are carefully chosen; they did not put to flight the enemy (for they were defeated), but the enemy's pride (by their brave and unexpected resistance) aye, standing their trial for valour or cowardice, hoarded not their lives but made Death their impartial umpire, that the Greeks might not wear the yoke of slavery and have hated tyranny on either hand.cf. Od. 3. 486 But the bones of those that have borne the brunt lie in the bosom of their country;the bones were collected after cremation by Antipater, presumably for sending to Athens (Polyb. 5. 10. 4) for it is the judgment of Zeus unto men that to make no slip in life and accomplish all things is for Gods, and He hath not granted unto man to escape destiny.the Greeks were defeated by Philip of Macedon at Chaeronea in Boeotia in 338 B.C.; cf. Aristid. i. 592, Liban. Ep. 1554, Themist. Or. 22. 335, Sch. Greg. Naz. Or. in Jul. 169 d [ *simwni/dhs ], Suid. suggnw/mona , Phalar. Ep. 126" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/51>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 51 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "T, H, R, A, S, Y, M, A, C, H, U, S do make my name; Chalcedon gave me to the day, And wisdom was my food and fame." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/88>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 88 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Says horse to ass ‘Kick not against the goad.’" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/64>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 64 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "and of the Priapean city which fronts the Bosporus" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/76>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 76 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "because mules are the offspring of asses." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/40>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 40 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "After rime Is the Southwind's time." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/52>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 52 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Call no man happy ere you have seen how his last day is ended." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2/text/9>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 9 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "su/nqeton or combination," .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/5>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 5 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This is the far-shooting Lord Apollo Son of Leto, and about him the Muses, whom he leadeth in a delightful dance." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/19>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 19 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "I was given to the Offspring of Leto by Euergus of Naxos son of Byzes, who was the first to make tiles of stone." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/16>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 16 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Another11. 57-70 are also in Ael. H.A. 16. 24, cf. 11. 36 is the child of a dainty long-maned Mare; she refuseth menial tasks and toil; she'll neither set hand to mill nor take up sieve, nor cast forth the muck, nor, for that she shunneth the soot, will she sit beside the oven. She taketh a mate only of necessity. Every day will she wash herself twice, or even thrice, and anointeth her with unguents. She ever weareth her hair deep-combed and wreathed with flowers. Such a wife may be a fair sight for other men, but she's an ill to her husband if he be not a despot or a king, such as take pride in adornments like to her." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/28>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 28 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "<If so,> no man would have so feared a lion in the shadowy hills nor yet a leopard if he met him alone in a narrow way." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/7>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 7 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "No longer care I for melodious hymns, no longer for the dance.this use of molph/ ‘song-dance’ as in the Iliad , indicates a very ancient author, e.g. Archilochus" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/74>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 74 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "This image of his brother was dedicated by Polystratus as an immortal monument of his mortal body." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/62>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 62 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "O Time, divine arch-overseer of every sort of things in the world of man, be thou messenger of our fate to all men, and tell how we died in the renowned plains of Boeotia because we sought to save the sacred land of Greece.ascribed in the mss to Gaetulicus, but in part surviving in letters of 350 —300 B.C. on a stone found near the Olympieum at Athens: prob. ref. to Chaeronea" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/50>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 50 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "The lines are of Parrhasius, the workmanship of Mys; I represent the lofty Troy which was taken by the sons of Aeacus.i.e. Achilles and Neoptolemus; lines means the design; cf. Paus. i. 28. 2" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/75>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 75 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Fitz-Stinkardsprob. a mock-patronymic on the father's side" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/87>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 87 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Well said the ox when he saw the saddle ‘This is not mine; I bid it a very good day.’cf. Hesych. a)stra/bh" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/51>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 51 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Modesty sitteth on the knowing eyelid, and effrontery on the unknowing; and wise is any that hath learnt this." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/60>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 60 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "or Gallus ; thus Semonides." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/63>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 63 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "blooming with all the good things that make a city flourish" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2/text/6>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 6 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "But afterwards when she was quite the general I saw her turned Helot and playing the harlot and walking with a strut.reading and translation doubtful; some edd. connect with fr. 4" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/2>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 2 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "... Medea is seated in a chair with Jason standing on her right and Aphrodite on her left, with the following inscription:" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/18>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 18 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "... Now we could by no means guess who was the maker of the Chest, but as for the author of the inscriptions upon it, while they may, of course, be the work of some other man, my impressions on the whole point to Eumelus of Corinth, particularly in view of his Processional to Delos .see Lyra Graeca i. p. 14; the date are against P.'s view" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/17>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 17 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Another cometh of an Ape; she is the greatest ill of all Zeus giveth man. Foul of face, such a woman maketh laughter for all men as she goeth through the town; short in neck, she moveth hardly, hipless, leanshanked —alas for the wretched man that claspeth such a mischief! Like an ape she knoweth all arts and wiles, nor recketh of men's laughter. Neither will she do a man any kindness; all her care, all her considering, is how she shall do the greatest ill she may." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/29>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 29 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "cuttlefish with tunnies, prawns with gudgeonsdo not mate?" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/8>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 8 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Men of Athens no longer recognise men of Megara.cf. Ibid. 2" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/73>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 73 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Eucoline" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/61>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 61 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Apollo begat two sons, Asclepius and Plato, the one to save the body and the other the soul.cf. Diog. L. 3. 45, Suid. s. sa/ou" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/66>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 66 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "and many are the waves of the wide sea that the Southwind rolls between us." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/78>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 78 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Aenyra is a place in Thrace called after Aenyrus." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/42>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 42 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Season, not soil, Rewards thy toil.cf. Theophr. C.P. 3. 23" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/54>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 54 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Not even a woman is so wanting in good wits as to choose the worse when she may have the better." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/30>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 30 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "I saw Epicharis on the way to Marathon" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/61>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 61 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "booty of the Mysians" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/3>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 3 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Jason weds Medea, and Aphrodite counsels them." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2/text/7>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 7 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/2> ;
      zoo:text "and any keblh/puris of the dunghill of Themistocles;" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/1>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 1 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Thundering Zeus, lad, hath the ends of all things there be, and doeth with them what he will. There's no mind in us men, but we live each day as it cometh like grazing cattle, knowing no whit how God shall end it. Yet Hope and Trust keep us all a-pondering the impracticable; some abide till a day come, others for the turning of years. There 's none alive but thinketh he will come home winged with wealth and good things next year; yet one of us ere he reach his goal is taken with unenvied Age, another's mind is wasted by miserable Disease, or Death sendeth him below dark Earth whelmed by War. Some die at sea when they have laden a ship with their substance, confounded by storm and the many waves of the purple brine; others tie a noose about their miserable neck and leave the sunlight of their free choice. So true is it that nothing is without ills, nay, ten thousand the Dooms of men, and their woes and sorrows past reckoning. If they would be advised by me, we should not set our hearts on good things, nor yet do ourselves despite by letting our minds dwell upon evil troubles." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/19>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 19 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "For this is the greatest ill that Zeus hath made, women. Even though they may seem to advantage us, a wife is more than all else a mischief to him that possesseth her; for whoso dwelleth with a woman, he never passeth a whole day glad, nor quickly shall he thrust out of doors Hunger the hated housefellow and hostile deity. But when a man thinketh withindoors to be gladdest at heart by grace of God or favour of man, then of all times will she find cause for blame and gird herself for battle. For where a woman is, they e'en cannot receive a stranger heartily. And she that most seemeth to be discreet, she is all the time doing the greatest harm; her husband is all agape for her, but the neighbours rejoice that yet another is deceived. And no man but will praise his own wife when he speaketh of her, not when he remembers her after her death (which would be put more explicitly) and blame another's, yet we cannot see or, emending the Gk. we know well that we be all alike. Aye, this is the greatest ill that Zeus hath made, this hath he put about us as the bondage of a fetter irrefragable, ever since Death received them that went a-warring for a woman.Helen, cf. Hes. Op. 165 ff" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/29>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 29 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Child of Zeus,prob. Aphrodite receive from Ecphantus this perfect offering; for it was to Thee he prayed as he finished painting it.or finished it by painting it; inscribed on a column" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/17>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 17 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "The Locrian Ajax draggeth Cassandra from Athena." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/18>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 18 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Another of a Bee; and happy he that getteth her. On her alone alighteth there no blame, and life doth flourish and increase because of her; loving and loved groweth she old with her husband, the mother of a fair and name-honoured progeny; she is pre-eminent among all the women, and a divine grace pervadeth her; neither taketh she delight in sitting among women where they tell tales of venery. Such wives are the best and wisest that Zeus bestoweth upon men; these other kinds, thanks unto Him, both are and will ever be a mischief in the world." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/9>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 9 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Though the Gods have given a fluteplayer wits, his wits fly away when he blows his flutes." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/62>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 62 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "Demosthenes in the speech On behalf of Ctesiphon; a proverb runs thus, which according to Demon in the 1st Book of his treatise On Proverbs takes its origin from the inroads of the border peoples and robbers into Mysia during the absence of King Telephus. Notable users of the proverb are Strattis in his Medea and Semonides in his Iambi. the proverb was used of anything that may be plundered with impunity" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/72>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 72 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "Thy body, Callisto, is wrapt in the bosom of earth, but thou hast left with thy friends the memory of thy virtues." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/77>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 77 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "the road to Aenyra" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4/text/60>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 60 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/4> ;
      zoo:text "By my counsels Sparta hath been shorn of her glory, holy Messene receiveth her children after long years, Megalopolis hath been crowned with the arms of Thebes, and all Greece ruleth herself in freedom.cf. Ibid. 9. 12. 6, Aristid. 2. 541, Sch. Aeschin. 3. 211, Plut. Non posse 1098 a, Cic. T.D. 5. 49" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/89>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 89 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "The wolf has come to call the goats from fold;" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/53>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 53 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "Never say that any mortal man is all-happy ere thou see how a fate without trouble hath come upon him at the last." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/65>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 65 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "that is, Priapus on the Hellespont, a city which they say was founded by Priapus son of Dionysus and Percote.cf. Str. 13, 587, 590" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5/text/41>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 41 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/5> ;
      zoo:text "The Southwest shifts 'tween cloud and clear, The Northwest nought but cloud doth bear." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1/text/50>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 50 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/SEMONIDES_AMORGINUS/elegy_and_iambus_semonides/e/1> ;
      zoo:text "a thick plaidie" .


